<p class="lead-p">If your yard still runs on halogen bulbs, the LED vs halogen landscape lighting question comes down to more than a lower electric bill. LEDs use roughly 75–80% less power, last ten times longer or more, run far cooler and let you shrink transformers and wire. Halogen still has a few things going for it, mainly a low purchase price and beautiful color. This guide compares the two on every point that matters to a Texas homeowner, works through the real cost at Texas electricity rates, and explains when a simple lamp swap makes sense and when it is time for new fixtures.</p>

<p>Most systems installed before about 2012 were built around 12-volt halogen lamps, usually the MR16 bi-pin spot or flood and the small T3 or JC capsule in path lights. Plenty of those systems are still in the ground, and many of them can be converted. Others are worn out and are better replaced. Knowing the difference saves money either way.</p>

<h2>LED vs Halogen at a Glance</h2>

<p>Here is how a typical 20-watt MR16 halogen lamp compares with the 4–5 watt LED lamp that replaces it in most landscape fixtures.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>20W MR16 halogen</th><th>4–5W LED (MR16 or integrated)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Power draw</td><td>20W (35W and 50W also common)</td><td>4–5W for similar light output</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rated life</td><td>About 2,000–4,000 hours</td><td>About 30,000–50,000 hours (to 70% output)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lens and housing heat</td><td>Very hot; can scorch foliage and burn fingers</td><td>Warm at the heat sink, safe to touch at the lens</td></tr>
<tr><td>Color rendering (CRI)</td><td>~100, the reference standard</td><td>80–95+ depending on quality</td></tr>
<tr><td>Color consistency</td><td>Very consistent lamp to lamp</td><td>Varies by brand and binning; good lamps match well</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dimming at end of run</td><td>Dims and yellows with low voltage</td><td>Many hold output across a wide voltage range</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upfront lamp cost</td><td>Low</td><td>Higher, though prices have fallen sharply</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical failure</td><td>Filament burns out suddenly</td><td>Slow fade, or driver failure from heat or moisture</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<h2>Energy Use: Why LEDs Win by a Wide Margin</h2>

<p>A halogen lamp makes light by heating a tungsten filament, so most of the electricity it draws becomes heat rather than light. An LED converts a much larger share of its power into visible light. In practical terms, a 4–5W LED MR16 produces about the same useful beam as a 20W halogen MR16, and a 7–8W LED can stand in for a 35W halogen.</p>

<p>That is the source of the 75–80% savings figure you see quoted. It holds up well in the field because landscape lighting runs for many hours every night, every night of the year. A small difference per fixture becomes a large difference across 20 or 30 fixtures and 365 evenings.</p>

<?= figure('patio-lighting', 'Texas patio and backyard lit with warm LED landscape lighting at night', 'Long Texas evenings outdoors mean long run times, which is exactly where LED efficiency adds up.') ?>

<h2>Lamp Life and the Hidden Cost of Replacing Bulbs</h2>

<p>A halogen MR16 is typically rated for about 2,000 to 4,000 hours. If your lights run from dusk to 11 p.m., that is roughly 2,000 hours a year, so you can expect to replace many lamps every one to two years. Run dusk to dawn and halogens may not last a single year.</p>

<p>Quality LED lamps and integrated fixtures are commonly rated for 30,000 to 50,000 hours. That rating usually means the point where output has dropped to 70% of new (called L70), not the point where the light goes dark. At six hours a night, 40,000 hours is more than 18 years of use.</p>

<p>The lamp itself is only part of the replacement cost. Each burned-out halogen means a walk around the yard, opening fixtures, handling hot glass, replacing gaskets that have hardened, and sometimes discovering corroded sockets. If you pay someone to do it, the service call often costs more than the bulbs.</p>

<h2>Heat: A Bigger Deal in Texas Than Elsewhere</h2>

<p>Halogen lamps run hot enough to brown nearby leaves, melt mulch-level plastics and cause burns if someone grabs a path light. In a Texas August, with ground and air temperatures already high, that heat also cooks gaskets and wire insulation inside the fixture, which is one reason old halogen fixtures leak and corrode.</p>

<p>LEDs are not heat-free. The light itself is cool, but the electronics behind the chip do produce heat, and heat is the main enemy of LED lifespan. That is why quality matters: a good LED lamp or fixture has a real aluminum or brass heat sink, and a well-designed fixture gives that heat somewhere to go. A bargain LED MR16 sealed into a tight, dark-bronze housing on a south-facing wall can fail early in Texas summers.</p>

<?= callout('<p>If you retrofit with drop-in LED lamps, choose ones rated for enclosed fixtures and outdoor use. Lamps that are fine in a ventilated kitchen fixture can overheat inside a sealed landscape housing.</p>', 'tip') ?>

<h2>Light Quality, CRI and Color Consistency</h2>

<h3>Color rendering</h3>
<p>Halogen has a color rendering index (CRI) of about 100, which is why stone, brick and foliage look rich under it. Early LEDs had a poor reputation here, with greenish or flat light. Modern landscape-grade LEDs typically rate CRI 80 to 90, and premium lamps reach 90 or above. For landscape work, CRI 90+ with a strong red value (often listed as R9) keeps red brick, crape myrtle blooms and warm limestone looking natural.</p>

<h3>Color temperature</h3>
<p>Halogen sits around 2800–3000K and warms further as it dims. LEDs are sold in fixed color temperatures, most often 2700K and 3000K for residential landscapes. That choice is a design decision; our <a href="/landscape-lighting-color-temperature-guide/">color temperature guide</a> covers which works best on stone, brick, plants and water.</p>

<h3>Lamp-to-lamp consistency</h3>
<p>Every halogen lamp of a given type looks essentially the same. LEDs can vary from batch to batch and brand to brand. Better manufacturers sort chips tightly (look for 3-step or tighter MacAdam binning) so a row of path lights matches. Mixing brands, or mixing new LEDs with a few leftover halogens, is the most common reason a retrofitted yard looks patchy.</p>

<?= figure('architectural-uplighting', 'Stone facade of a Texas home grazed with warm LED uplights', 'High-CRI, warm-white LEDs keep limestone and brick looking the way they do in daylight.') ?>

<h2>Upfront Cost: Where Halogen Still Looks Cheaper</h2>

<p>A halogen MR16 costs very little, and a basic halogen fixture is inexpensive too. A good LED lamp costs several times as much, and a quality integrated LED fixture costs more than a basic halogen fixture. On the shelf, halogen wins.</p>

<p>Over the life of the system it does not. Once you add electricity, replacement lamps and labor, LEDs are almost always cheaper within a few years. For a full planning range on a new professional system, which typically runs $2,500–$12,000 for most Texas homes, see our <a href="/landscape-lighting-cost-calculator/">landscape lighting cost calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>Worked Example: Running Cost at Texas Electricity Rates</h2>

<p>Here is the math for a common setup: 20 fixtures, each with a 20W halogen MR16, compared with the same 20 fixtures on 5W LEDs. We will use 15¢ per kWh, a reasonable planning figure for many Texas residential plans (check your own bill, since rates vary by provider and plan).</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Dusk to 11 p.m.:</strong> about 6 hours a night × 365 = 2,190 hours a year.</li>
<li><strong>Dusk to dawn:</strong> about 11 hours a night on average × 365 = 4,015 hours a year.</li>
<li><strong>Formula:</strong> watts × hours ÷ 1,000 = kWh; kWh × $0.15 = cost.</li>
</ul>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Halogen (20 × 20W)</th><th>LED (20 × 5W)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Total lamp load</td><td>400W</td><td>100W</td></tr>
<tr><td>kWh per year, 6 h/night</td><td>400 × 2,190 ÷ 1,000 = 876 kWh</td><td>100 × 2,190 ÷ 1,000 = 219 kWh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost per year, 6 h/night</td><td>876 × $0.15 = $131.40</td><td>219 × $0.15 = $32.85</td></tr>
<tr><td>kWh per year, dusk to dawn</td><td>400 × 4,015 ÷ 1,000 = 1,606 kWh</td><td>100 × 4,015 ÷ 1,000 = 401.5 kWh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost per year, dusk to dawn</td><td>1,606 × $0.15 = $240.90</td><td>401.5 × $0.15 = $60.23</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lamp replacements per year (6 h/night)</td><td>Roughly half to all 20 lamps</td><td>Typically none</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<p>On the six-hour schedule the LED system saves about $98 a year in electricity; dusk to dawn, about $181. Transformers also lose some energy as heat, and that loss is proportionally larger on a big halogen load, so real-world savings are usually a little higher than the lamp-only math. Add the halogen lamps you no longer buy and install, and a typical LED retrofit pays for itself in a few years. Larger properties with 40 or 60 fixtures see the savings scale accordingly.</p>

