<h2>What Switching to LED Does to Transformer Sizing</h2>

<p>Low-voltage transformers are sized by total watts, and good practice is to load them to no more than about 80% of their rating. The halogen system above draws 400W, so it needs at least a 500W transformer. The same layout on LEDs draws 100W, which a 150W or 200W transformer handles with room to spare.</p>

<p>Lower wattage also means lower current. At 12 volts, 400W is about 33 amps, while 100W is about 8 amps. Less current means far less voltage drop on long wire runs, which is why LED systems can use smaller gauge wire, longer runs or fewer home runs than halogen systems. If you are converting, your existing transformer will almost always be more than big enough; the question is whether its taps are now set too high. An over-voltage LED lamp runs hot and dies early. Our <a href="/low-voltage-landscape-lighting-guide/">low-voltage lighting guide</a> explains taps, wire gauge and voltage drop in detail.</p>

