<h2>Retrofit or Replace? Choosing the Right Upgrade</h2>

<h3>Drop-in LED lamps</h3>
<p>A drop-in lamp replaces the halogen bulb in the existing fixture: an LED MR16 for spotlights, or an LED bi-pin (G4) or wedge lamp for many path lights. It is the lowest-cost way to cut energy use and works well when the fixtures themselves are in good shape.</p>

<h3>Integrated LED fixtures</h3>
<p>An integrated fixture has the LED and driver built in and sealed at the factory. Heat sinking is designed as one system, there is no socket to corrode, and many models let you adjust output or swap optics for a different beam angle. The trade-off is that at the very end of its life you replace the fixture or its LED module rather than a bulb. Given 30,000+ hour ratings, that is a long way off.</p>

<h3>When retrofitting makes sense</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fixtures are solid brass, copper or heavy cast aluminum with intact finishes and lenses.</li>
<li>Sockets are clean and green-free, and gaskets can be replaced.</li>
<li>The layout and aiming still suit the landscape as it has grown.</li>
<li>The wire and connections test well and the transformer is healthy.</li>
</ul>

<h3>When replacing fixtures is the better call</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fixtures are thin painted aluminum or plastic with peeling finish, cracked lenses or heavy corrosion, which is common near the Gulf Coast.</li>
<li>Sockets are corroded or the fixture fills with water after rain or irrigation.</li>
<li>Trees have outgrown the original design and the lights need to move anyway.</li>
<li>You want features drop-ins cannot give, such as tighter glare control, adjustable output or smart zone control.</li>
</ul>

<p>Many Texas projects end up as a mix: good brass fixtures get new LED lamps, failing ones are replaced with integrated LEDs, and the transformer and connections are brought up to standard at the same visit. Our <a href="/services/landscape-lighting-maintenance/">maintenance and repair service</a> handles LED retrofits on any brand of system.</p>

