<h2>Is There Still a Case for Halogen?</h2>

<p>Very little. Halogen lamps are getting harder to find as manufacturers move on, and their color advantage has largely closed with high-CRI LEDs. The only real argument is a small system that runs a few hours a week, where the upfront cost of new lamps takes years to pay back. For a typical Texas yard that lights up every evening, LED is the clear choice. At Landscape Lighting Texas, new installations are designed around professional-grade LED fixtures.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Can I put LED bulbs in my existing halogen landscape fixtures?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes. Most 12-volt halogen MR16 and bi-pin fixtures accept LED replacement lamps of the same base. Choose lamps rated for outdoor, enclosed use, match the beam angle to what the fixture was doing before, and check that the transformer taps are not now delivering too much voltage.</p>

<h3>How much will I save by switching from halogen to LED?</h3>
<p>Expect roughly 75–80% lower lighting energy use. For 20 fixtures running six hours a night at 15¢ per kWh, that is about $98 a year in electricity, plus the cost of replacement halogen lamps. Run your own numbers with the <a href="/tools/energy-savings-calculator/">LED energy savings calculator</a>.</p>

<h3>Do LED landscape lights look as good as halogen?</h3>
<p>Quality ones do. Choose warm color temperatures (2700K or 3000K) and CRI 90 or higher, and stick to one brand or matched lamps across the property so the color stays consistent.</p>

<h3>Do I need a new transformer when I switch to LED?</h3>
<p>Rarely for capacity, since LED loads are much smaller. You may need to rewire taps to lower voltage, and an older electronic transformer may need replacing if it flickers with a light load. A failing or corroded transformer should be replaced either way.</p>

